    [Doc][Misc] Add fullscreen support for overflowing tables (#18072)
    
    ### What this PR does / why we need it?
    
    Wide documentation tables can be difficult to inspect within the normal
    content column. This PR adds a fullscreen action only when a table
    overflows horizontally.
    
    - Places a subtle action icon at the table’s top-right corner without
    changing its normal layout, following Material’s clipboard button
    styling.
    - Displays the selected table in fullscreen with independent horizontal
    and vertical scrolling.
    - Keeps the table header, including multi-row headers, and the first
    column visible while scrolling.
    - Disables document scrolling during fullscreen and supports exiting
    through the close button or Esc, restoring focus to the opener.
    - Supports light and dark themes and hides fullscreen controls when
    printing.
    
    #### How to use this function?
    Click the button below to enter full-screen mode:
    <img width="1448" height="412" alt="image"
    src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/51ba01e3-e77e-4fe3-ba95-64373f79e9c9"
    />
    
    Full-screen effect demo image:
    
    https://vllm--18072.org.readthedocs.build/projects/ascend/en/18072/user_guide/support_matrix/feature_matrix.html
    <img width="2561" height="1315" alt="image"
    src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/6dca15a3-233d-4272-a012-4641ccf74180"
    />
    
    ### Does this PR introduce _any_ user-facing change?
    
    Yes. Horizontally overflowing tables show a fullscreen action. Tables
    that fit within the available width keep their existing behavior and
    show no action.
    
    ### How was this patch tested?
    
    - Built documentation locally with `.venv/bin/mkdocs build --strict
    --clean`.
    - Verified fullscreen interaction on the Supported Models page in
    Chrome.
    - Verified that fullscreen styles apply to both plain tables and tables
    with a class while overriding Material’s default table styles.
    
    AI assistance: Implementation and validation were assisted by OpenAI
    Codex.
    
    
    - vLLM main:
    https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/commit/ced6857afa0ea7b2e3f0846a62e1394e90f15607
    
